<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amanullah Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1892–1960</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amanullah Khan ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> from 1919 to 1929, first as emir and from 1926 as king. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-afghan-war">Third Anglo-Afghan War</a> in 1919 and won full control of foreign policy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. His rapid modernising reforms, including dress codes and education for women, provoked a rebellion that forced him to abdicate in 1929, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan.jpg" alt="Amanullah Khan" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Amanullah_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
